//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 76 - NVI SMART-MONEY VOLUME REGIME. A GENUINELY NEW MECHANISM, BARE.
//
// The stored scheduled prompt describes a board state ("Attack 37, build its filter stack") more than
// seventy-five attacks stale. THE DOCS OVERRIDE IT, AGAIN, per the prompt's own standing instruction:
// Attack 41 closed Attack 37 on cost, Attack 43 closed the whole sweep-reversal family, Attack 68 (Attack
// 66 + OBV divergence-magnitude floor) is the board's strongest both-halves candidate, and Attack 75's own
// queue item 8 asked for one further genuinely new mechanism, distinct from every family already on the
// board. This build is that instruction, continuing numbering after Attack 75, not Attack 37.
//
// ==================== WHAT THIS CLAIMS, IN ONE SENTENCE ====================
// The Negative Volume Index (NVI) is a cumulative series that only accumulates a bar's percentage price
// change on bars where volume FELL from the prior bar, and holds flat on every bar where volume rose --
// Fosback's "smart money trades on quiet volume" claim, applied here at bar level instead of the classic
// daily-close level. When NVI crosses above its own long moving average, price return has been
// accumulating disproportionately on LOW-participation bars relative to its own trend -- a regime where
// quiet, low-volume drift is carrying price up rather than crowded, high-volume participation -- and that
// regime tends to persist because it reflects a change in WHO is driving price, not a same-instrument
// technical level.
//
// ==================== WHY THIS IS A NEW INPUT CLASS FOR THIS LAB ====================
// STRATEGY-LEDGER's "families still open" list names "volume/participation profile" as untried and
// distinct from "order-flow imbalance proxies" (Attack 53, a same-window pressure SUM) and from the OBV
// divergence family (Attack 66-71, a point-to-point comparison of a cumulative volume series against
// price AT TWO CONFIRMED PIVOTS). NVI is neither: it is a cumulative series that is CONDITIONALLY GATED
// by whether volume itself rose or fell bar-to-bar, entirely independent of price direction or any pivot
// structure, then compared only to ITS OWN moving average -- the same shape as a price/EMA trend filter,
// but built from a volume-gated return series instead of price. No prior BTC attack has used ta.nvi or
// ta.pvi. Genuinely distinct from every mechanism on the board: not a price channel (33), not a calendar
// anchor (34/35/50/54/55/74), not a volatility state (6/36/65), not a swing-level tap or failed break
// (37-47, 59, 63, 71/72's autocorrelation persistence is a return-sign statistic, not a volume-gated
// return series), not a fixed grid or single-bar impulse (48/49/75), not a price/oscillator or
// price/OBV divergence (51/52/66-71), not order-flow absorption (53), not a streak or crossover (56-58),
// not Ichimoku (73).
//
// ==================== MECHANICS, NO ARRAYS, NO USER-DEFINED FUNCTIONS ====================
// nvi = ta.nvi (allowlisted, engine-native cumulative series). nviEma = ta.ema(nvi, nviEmaLen). The
// ENTRY TRIGGER is ta.crossover(nvi, nviEma) -- the regime turning bullish is itself the signal, not a
// filter bolted onto something else (Attack 15's lesson: the signal's own terms deserve the same scrutiny
// as filters, so this cycle puts the volume-regime term IN the trigger position, where it is maximally
// exposed to the kill rule). The FLIP RESPONSE (standing objective 2) is the mirror crossunder: if a long
// position is open when ta.crossunder(nvi, nviEma) fires, it is closed immediately via strategy.close,
// flattening on the regime change rather than waiting for the fixed stop or target to be hit.
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg:
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) - EXCLUSION via rBig on rLong = close - swingLow, never clamped.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) - slPx = ta.lowest(low, swingLen)[1], a genuine prior swing low
//        computed from bars STRICTLY BEFORE the signal bar (the [1] shift, same convention as Attack 37/59/
//        63/66/71), never the NVI trigger level itself and never the entry bar's own low.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) - LONG ONLY this cycle, following this lab's established convention for
//        a first pass on a new mechanism (Attacks 50/59/63/65/66/72/73/74/75 all opened one-sided). The
//        short leg (NVI crossing BELOW its EMA as a bearish-regime short trigger, its own geometry, never
//        mirrored) is deferred and reported as OUTSTANDING -- this is an INTERIM result, not a finished one.
//   BINDING (E17) - justTurnedBull (the NVI/EMA crossover) AND rBig AND haveSwing must all independently
//        bind; this is the first run of this mechanism and two credits are budgeted this cycle for the
//        H1/H2 pair (H2 spent only if H1 clears the kill rule), so no per-term counter build was
//        affordable -- the realized trade count against the pre-run frequency estimate below is the
//        binding evidence this cycle can afford, and a per-term counter build is queued if this clears
//        the kill rule and earns a filter stack.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) - justTurnedBull reads a VOLUME-GATED CUMULATIVE RETURN series compared to its own
//        average (no price-level component); swingLow reads a PRICE LEVEL from a distinct rolling window;
//        rBig reads a PRICE DISTANCE from entry to that level. Three independent quantities -- volume-
//        participation state, price level, price distance -- none repeats another.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8, fourth-confirmation check) - the entry trigger IS the crossover itself,
//        not a filter phrased as "already true for N bars" paired with a separate trigger, so the
//        generalized same-bar-conjunction failure mode does not apply: there is exactly one transition
//        event (ta.crossover(nvi, nviEma)) gating entry, checked against rBig/haveSwing which are ordinary
//        bar-by-bar conditions, not competing latches.
//   WEEKENDS (structural note 4) - NVI/PVI accumulate through weekend bars exactly as they do on weekdays;
//        this mechanism has no calendar dependency at all, so weekends are handled by construction, not by
//        a special-case rule -- stated explicitly per the standing requirement rather than left implicit.
//   CASCADE (HARD LESSON 42/43) - LONG at 100% equity, single entry id "L", pyramiding=1; expect
//        cascadeRatio 1 / maxCascadeDepth 1 on both halves, confirmed below.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY (2R, lab convention; HARD LESSON 13 found the R:R axis neutral, so no tuning
//        credit spent hunting "the best" multiple on a first pass). The regime-flip flatten is a
//        strategy.close signal exit, not a modification of the fixed SL/TP prices themselves -- same
//        pattern as the retired VWAP base's "flip signal: close crossing VWAP" rule.
//
// ==================== FREQUENCY ESTIMATE, REGISTERED BEFORE RUNNING (HARD LESSON 4) ====================
// NVI/EMA(50) crossovers on a 15m series are a genuinely untested quantity in this lab -- no prior attack
// has plotted this series. Anchored loosely between a slow calendar cadence (Attack 34: 30/23 trades) and
// a fast oscillator cross (Attack 57's raw EMA crossover: 2,154 hits before any filter, on one half): a
// 50-bar EMA of a cumulative series should cross far less often than a raw price EMA cross, but with no
// direct precedent the estimate carries unusually wide error bars. Pre-registering **80-300 trades per
// half**, explicitly flagged as low-confidence given the total absence of a comparable prior measurement.
//
// ==================== OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17), PER THE PROMPT'S OWN KILL RULE ====
//   * H1 (never-tuned, 2022-01-01 to 2024-06-08) PROFIT FACTOR BELOW 1.0 -> DISCARD immediately. Do not
//     run H2. Do not add filters to rescue H1.
//   * H1 clears 1.0 -> run H2 (2024-06-08 to 2026-09-01) as the second and last credit this cycle.
//   * BOTH halves clear 1.0, trades roughly 60-350 per half -> a genuine both-halves candidate, joining
//     Attack 37/46/66-68 on the board; report avg loser, win rate and max DD alongside PF (the board's
//     three-drawdown-category taxonomy), and queue it for a filter stack exactly as Attack 37/66 earned
//     theirs.
//   * H1 clears but H2 does not -> report both, DISCARD as a candidate (per Attack 53/72/74's precedent:
//     this would be the fourth "helps H1, breaks H2" result), not as evidence the volume-participation
//     family as a whole is dead.
//   * FEWER THAN ~30 TRADES in a half -> that half's ratio is a DIRECTION per LESSON 12, not a result.
//   * A LARGE SHARE OF HOLDS AT maxBars 480 -> the cap is truncating (HARD LESSON 38); report before
//     drawing any conclusion.
//   REPORT GROSS, COMMISSION, ACHIEVED WIN/LOSS RATIO, AVG LOSER AND WIN RATE alongside net (LESSONS 36/39
//     and the board's own three-drawdown-category taxonomy).

strategy("BTC Attack76 NVI Smart-Money Regime", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

nviEmaLen = input.int(50, "NVI regime EMA length")
swingLen  = input.int(20, "Structural swing-low lookback (bars)")
minRpct   = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
targetR   = input.float(2.0,  "Target, multiple of R (lab convention)")
maxBars   = input.int(480,    "Max bars in trade -- 5 days on 15m")

nvi    = ta.nvi
nviEma = ta.ema(nvi, nviEmaLen)

justTurnedBull = ta.crossover(nvi, nviEma)
justTurnedBear = ta.crossunder(nvi, nviEma)

swingLow  = ta.lowest(low, swingLen)[1]
haveSwing = not na(swingLow)

rLong = haveSwing ? close - swingLow : na
minR  = close * minRpct / 100
rBig  = not na(rLong) and rLong >= minR

flat   = strategy.position_size == 0
goLong = flat and justTurnedBull and haveSwing and rBig

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

if goLong
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx   := swingLow
    tpPx   := close + targetR * rLong
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if strategy.position_size > 0 and justTurnedBear
    strategy.close("L")
    barsIn := 0

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(nvi,    title="NVI")
plot(nviEma, title="NVI_EMA")
